==English==
{{wp}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɡɛt/|a=RP,GA,CA}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-get.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɡɪt/|q1=regionally restricted}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-git.ogg|a=US,regional}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɡet/|a=AU,NZ}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛt|ɪt|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:geten|id=obtain|tree=+}}
From {{inh|en|enm|geten}}, from {{der|en|non|geta}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*getaną|t=to find; to acquire, attain, get, hold, receive}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*gʰed-|t=to find; to hold; to seize; to take}}. 
{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|yol|get|t=to get}}, {{cog|da|gide|t=to care, like}}, {{cog|fo|gita|t=to be able}}, {{cog|is|geta|t=to be able; to beget, father; to achieve, obtain, to guess; to mention}}, {{cog|nn|gjeta}}, {{m|nn|gjete|t=to guess; to mention}}, {{cog|gmq-scy|gida|t=to have the energy to, to feel up for}}, {{m|gmq-scy|gæda|t=to guess}}, {{cog|sv|gita}}, {{m|sv|gitta|t=to be able, to bring oneself to, to care}}, {{cog|got|𐌱𐌹𐌲𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽|t=to discover, find}}; also {{cog|la|edera}}, {{m|la|hedera|t=ivy}}, {{m|la|praeda|t=booty, pillage, plunder; prey; gain, profit}}, {{m|la|prehendō}}, {{m|la|prēndō|t=to grab, grasp, seize; to attain, reach}}, {{cog|grc|χανδάνω|t=to comprise, contain, hold}}, {{cog|sq|gjej|t=to find}}. 
{{col-bottom}}

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|git||AAVE|Appalachian|Southern US}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|+|+|got,gat<l:Scotland,Northern England,archaic>|got,gotten<l:United States,Canada,more recently,_,UK,Ireland,Australia,New Zealand>,n<l:Geordie>}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|or|ditransitive}} To [[obtain]]; to [[acquire]].
#: {{syn|en|acquire|come by|have|Thesaurus:achieve|Thesaurus:acquire}}
#: {{ant|en|lose}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm going to '''get''' a computer tomorrow from the discount store.}}
#: {{ux|en|Lance is going to '''get''' Mary a ring.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[receive]].
#: {{syn|en|receive|be [[give]]n|Thesaurus:receive}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''got''' a computer from my parents for my birthday.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''got''' a severe reprimand for that.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|page=175|chapter=8|passage=Afore we got to the shanty Colonel Applegate stuck his head out of the door. His temper had been getting raggeder all the time, and the sousing he '''got''' when he fell overboard had just about ripped what was left of it to ravellings.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|in a perfect construction, with present-tense meaning}} To [[have]]. {{n-g|See usage notes.}}
#: {{uxa|en|I've '''got''' a concert ticket for you.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[fetch]], [[bring]], [[take]].
#: {{syn|en|bring|fetch|retrieve}}
#: {{ux|en|Can you '''get''' my bag from the living-room, please?}}
#: {{ux|en|I need to '''get''' this to the office.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Genesis|31|13|passage='''Get''' thee out from this land.}}
#* {{RQ:Knolles Turkes|page=634|passage=He{{...}}'''got''' himself{{...}}to the strong town of Mega.}}
# {{senseid|en|become}} {{lb|en|copulative|rather|informal|followed by an adjective}} To {{l|en|become|id=copulative}}, or cause oneself to become {{q|often with temporary states, past participle adjectives and comparatives}}.
#: {{par|en|become<id:copulative>|turn<id:become>|go<id:become>|come<id:become>|fall<id:become>|grow<id:become>|wax<id:become>}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm '''getting''' hungry; how about you?}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm going out to '''get''' drunk.}}
#* '''November 1, 1833''', {{w|Samuel Taylor Coleridge}}, ''Table Talk''
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|page=175|chapter=8|passage=Afore we got to the shanty Colonel Applegate stuck his head out of the door. His temper had been '''getting''' raggeder all the time, and the sousing he got when he fell overboard had just about ripped what was left of it to ravellings.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[cause]] to become; to bring about.
#: {{syn|en|cause to [[be]]|cause to [[become]]|make}}
#: {{ux|en|That song '''gets''' me so depressed every time I hear it.}}
#: {{ux|en|I'll '''get''' this finished by lunchtime.}}
#: {{ux|en|I can't '''get''' these boots off.}}
#: {{ux|en|I can’t '''get''' my hands warm.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|page=6|chapter=1|passage=Then there came a reg'lar terror of a sou'wester same as you don't get one summer in a thousand, and blowed the shanty flat and ripped about half of the weir poles out of the sand. We spent consider'ble money '''getting''' 'em reset, and then a swordfish got into the pound and tore the nets all to slathers, right in the middle of the squiteague season.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[cause]] to do.
#: {{syn|en|make}}
#: {{ux|en|Somehow she '''got''' him to agree to it.}}
#: {{ux|en|I can't '''get''' it to work.}}
#: {{ux|en|I can't '''get''' it working.}}
#: {{ux|en|I can’t '''get''' my kids to go to bed early.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Twelfth Night|act=III|scene=iii|page=268|passage='''Get''' him to say his prayers.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to come or go or move.
#: {{ux|en|I '''got''' him to his room.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|with various prepositions, such as [[into]], [[over]], or [[behind]]; for specific idiomatic senses see individual entries [[get into]], [[get over]], etc.}} To [[adopt]], [[assume]], [[arrive]] at, or [[progress]] towards (a certain position, location, state).
#: {{syn|en|[[arrive]] at|reach}}
#: {{syn|en|go|move}}
#: {{ux|en|The actors are '''getting''' into position.}}
#: {{ux|en|When are we going to '''get''' to London?}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm '''getting''' into a muddle.}}
#: {{ux|en|We '''got''' behind the wall.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cover (a certain distance) while travelling.
#: {{ux|en|to '''get''' a mile}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|catenative}} (with [[full infinitive]] or [[gerund-participle]]) To [[begin]] (doing something or to do something).
#: {{syn|en|begin|commence|start|Thesaurus:begin}}
#: {{ux|en|We ought to '''get''' moving or we'll be late.}}
#: {{ux|en|After lunch we '''got''' chatting.}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm '''getting''' to like him better now.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[take]] or [[catch]] (a scheduled transportation service).
#: {{syn|en|catch|take}}
#: {{ux|en|I normally '''get''' the 7:45 train.}}
#: {{ux|en|I'll '''get''' the 9 a.m. [flight] to Boston.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To respond to (a [[telephone]] call, a [[doorbell]], etc).
#: {{syn|en|answer}}
#: {{ux|en|Can you '''get''' that call, please? I'm busy.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|catenative}} (with [[full infinitive]]) To be [[able]], be [[permitted]], or have the opportunity (to do something desirable or ironically implied to be desirable).
#: {{syn|en|be able to}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm so jealous that you '''got''' to see them perform live!}}
#: {{ux|en|The finders '''get''' to keep 80 percent of the treasure.}}
#: {{ux|en|Great. I '''get''' to clean the toilets today.}}
# {{senseid|en|understand}} {{lb|en|transitive|informal}} To [[understand]]. {{qualifier|compare {{m|en|get it}}}}
#: {{syn|en|dig|follow|make sense of|understand}}
#: {{ux|en|Yeah, I '''get''' it, it's just not funny.}}
#: {{ux|en|I don't '''get''' what you mean by "fun". This place sucks!}}
#: {{ux|en|I mentioned that I was feeling sad, so she mailed me a box of chocolates. She '''gets''' me.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|informal}} To be [[tell|told]]; be the [[recipient]] of (a question, comparison, opinion, etc.).
#: {{ux|en|"You look just like Helen Mirren." / "I '''get''' that a lot."}}
# {{lb|en|auxiliary|informal}} {{non-gloss|Used with the [[past participle]] to form the dynamic [[passive voice]] of a [[dynamic verb]]. Compared with static passive with ''to [[be]]'', this emphasizes the commencement of an action or entry into a state.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|to [[be]]}}{{ux|en|He '''got''' bitten by a dog.}}
# {{lb|en|impersonal|informal}} {{non-gloss|Used with a pronoun subject, usually {{m|en|you}} but sometimes {{m|en|one}}, to indicate that the object of the verb exists, can occur or is otherwise typical.}}
#: {{ux|en|You '''get''' some very rude people here.}}
#: {{ux|en|It was the kind of shop you used to '''get''' in most small towns.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To become [[ill]] with or [[catch]] (a [[disease]]).
#: {{syn|en|catch|come down with}}
#: {{ux|en|I went on holiday and '''got''' malaria.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|informal}} To [[catch out]], [[trick]] successfully.
#: {{syn|en|con|deceive|dupe|hoodwink|trick|Thesaurus:deceive}}
#: {{ux|en|He keeps calling pretending to be my boss&mdash;it '''gets''' me every time.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|informal}} To [[perplex]], [[stump]].
#: {{syn|en|confuse|perplex|stump}}
#: {{ux|en|That question's really '''got''' me.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To find as an answer.
#: {{syn|en|obtain}}
#: {{ux|en|What did you '''get''' for question four?}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|informal}} To bring to reckoning; to catch (usually as a criminal); to effect retribution.
#: {{syn|en|catch|nab|nobble|}}
#: {{ux|en|The cops finally '''got''' me.}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm gonna '''get''' him for that.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[hear]] completely; [[catch]].
#: {{syn|en|catch|hear}}
#: {{ux|en|Sorry, I didn't '''get''' that. Could you repeat it?}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[getter#Verb|getter]].
#: {{syn|en|getter}}
#: {{ux|en|I put the getter into the container to '''get''' the gases.}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare}} To [[beget]] (of a father).
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Othello|I|iii|page=314|passage=I had rather to adopt a child than '''get''' it.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|I|ii|page=4|passage=Thou poisonous slave, '''got''' by the devil himself / Upon thy wicked dam, come forth!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=w:Hilary Mantel|title=w:Wolf Hall|page=310|publisher=Fourth Estate|year_published=2010
|passage=Walter had said, dear God, Thomas, it was St fucking Felicity if I'm not mistaken, and her face was to the wall for sure the night I '''got''' you.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[learn]]; to commit to [[memory]]; to [[memorize]]; sometimes with ''out''.
#: {{ux|en|to '''get''' a lesson;&emsp; to '''get''' out one's Greek lesson}}
# {{lb|en|imperative|informal}} {{non-gloss|Used with a personal pronoun to indicate that someone is being [[pretentious]] or [[grandiose]].}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Get''' her with her new hairdo.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1966|author=Dorothy Fields|title=If My Friends Could See Me Now
|passage=Brother, '''get''' her! Draped on a bedspread made from three kinds of fur!}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2007|author=Tom Dyckhoff; [http://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2007/may/12/shopping.homes1 Let's move to ...]|journal=The Guardian
|passage=Money's pouring in somewhere, because Churchgate's got lovely new stone setts, and a cultural quarter (ooh, '''get''' her) is promised.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|informal|mostly|imperative}} To [[go]], to [[leave]]; to [[scram]].
#: {{syn|en|get out|go|leave|scram|Thesaurus:flee|Thesaurus:leave}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1991|author=Theodore Dreiser; T. D. Nostwich|title=Newspaper Days|publisher=University of Pennsylvania Press|isbn=9780812230956|page=663
|passage='''Get''', now — '''get'''! — before I call an officer and lay a charge against ye.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1952|author=Fredric Brown; Mack Reynolds|title=Me and Flapjack and the Martians
|passage=I had a sneaking suspicion that it wasn't no flashlight and I wasn't too curious, just then, to find out what would happen if he did more than wave it at me, so I '''got'''. I went back about twenty feet or so and watched.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Sarah Webb|title=The Loving Kind|publisher=Pan Macmillan|isbn=9780230749672
|passage=&#39;Go on, '''get'''. You look a state. We can&#39;t let Leo see you like that.&#39;}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Paul Zindel|title=Ladies at the Alamo|publisher=Graymalkin Media|isbn=9781935169741
|passage=Now go on, '''get'''! '''Get'''! '''Get'''! (''she chases Joanne out the door with the hammer.'')}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=April Daniels|title=Dreadnought|publisher=Diversion Books|isbn=9781682300671
|passage="{{...}} and then I'll switch over to the police band to know when the bacon's getting ready to stick its nose in. When I tell you to '''get''', you '''get''', understand?" Calamity asks as she retapes the earbud into her ear.}}
# {{lb|en|euphemism}} To [[kill]].
#: {{syn|en|assault|beat|beat up}}
#: {{ux|en|They’re coming to '''get''' you, Barbara.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To make [[acquisition]]s; to [[gain]]; to [[profit]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-1|IV|iii|page=112|passage=We mourn, France smiles; we lose, they daily '''get'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[measure]].
#: {{ux|en|Did you '''get''' her temperature?}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[cause]] someone to [[laugh]].
#: {{ux|en|It '''gets''' me every time!}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The meaning "to have" is found only in perfect tenses but has present meaning; hence "I have got" has the same meaning as "I have". (Sometimes the form ''had got'' is used to mean "had", as in "Luckily, I was able to start a fire because I had got a lighter in my pocket".) In speech and in all except formal writing, the word "have" is normally reduced to /v/ and spelled "-'ve" or, in American usage, dropped entirely (e.g. "I got a God-fearing woman, one I can easily afford", [[w:Slow Train (Bob Dylan song)|''Slow Train'']], Bob Dylan), leading to nonstandard usages such as "he gots" = "he has", "he doesn't got" = "he doesn't have".
* Some dialects (e.g. American English dialects) use both ''[[gotten]]'' and ''[[got]]'' as past participles, while others (e.g. dialects of Southern England) use only [[got]]. In dialects that use both, ''got'' is used for the meanings "to have" and "to have to", while ''gotten'' is used for all other meanings.<ref>[http://www-personal.umich.edu/~jlawler/aue/gotten.html http://www-personal.umich.edu/~jlawler/aue/gotten.html] and [http://www.miketodd.net/encyc/gotten.htm http://www.miketodd.net/encyc/gotten.htm]</ref> This allows for a distinction between "I've gotten a ticket" (I have received or obtained a ticket) vs. "I've got a ticket" (I currently have a ticket).
** Note that this does not apply to the related verbs {{m|en|beget}}, {{m|en|forget}}, whose past participles are always ''begotten'', ''forgotten''.
* "get" is one of the most common verbs in English, and the many meanings may be confusing for language learners. The following table indicates some of the different constructions found, along with the most common meanings of each:
{|class="wikitable"
! Construction !! Most common meanings !! Example
|-
| ''get'' + object || to [[receive]], to [[obtain]], to [[take]] || They got a cute cat today
|-
| ''have got'' + object || to [[have]], to [[possess]] || She has got a nice garden
|-
| ''have got'' + person || to [[have]] (as a [[relation]]) || I've got a sister / a friend / a boss
|-
| ''have got'' + ''to'' || to [[have to]] (as a [[must]]) || I've got to phone a friend
|-
| ''get'' + person || to [[understand]] ''or'' to [[catch]] || You get me? / I'll get you
|-
| ''get'' + concept || to [[understand]] || He began to get the idea
|-
| ''get'' + adjective || to [[become]] || She's getting taller quickly
|-
| ''get'' + person + adjective || to [[cause]] to [[become]] || They got him full with a big dinner
|-
| ''get'' + person + object || to [[give]] ''or'' to [[fetch]] || We got them two cats / Get me my shoes
|-
| ''get'' + location adverb || rowspan=2|to [[arrive]], to [[reach]] || We got far away from there
|-
| ''get'' + ''to'' + location || Soon she got to her destination
|-
| ''get'' + ''to'' + verb || to [[be able to]], to be [[permitted]] || You get to eat ice cream
|-
| ''get'' + person + ''to'' + verb || to [[cause]] to [[do]] || We get them to dance every time
|-
| ''get'' + verb + ''-ing'' || to [[begin]] [[doing]], to [[start]] || They got dancing right away
|-
| ''get'' + verb + ''-ed/-en'' (+ ''by'') || to [[be]] ([[passive voice]]) || They got energized by the music
|}

